1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,800
Pay off the mortgage or invest the money. This is

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:05,800
the single most argued about question I get, and most

3
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:08,800
of the answers you have heard are missing the part

4
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:11,800
that actually decides it. Let us set the table. Say

5
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:14,800
you have three hundred thousand dollars left on the loan,

6
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:17,800
your payment is about one thousand two hundred dollars a

7
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:20,800
month before taxes and insurance, and you have found an

8
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:23,800
extra five hundred dollars a month you could throw at

9
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:26,800
either side. The rate on the loan is the whole

10
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:29,800
argument. Right now the thirty year fixed average is six

11
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:32,800
point eight five percent. That is your guaranteed return for

12
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:35,800
paying the loan down early. Guaranteed is the word doing

13
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:38,800
the work there. On the other side, say you assume

14
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:41,800
seven percent from a broad market index fund. I want

15
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:44,800
to be careful here, because that is an assumption I

16
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:47,800
am making for the math, not a promise anybody made

17
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:50,800
to you. The market does not owe you seven percent

18
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:53,800
in any particular decade. So on paper it is close

19
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:56,800
to a coin flip, and that is exactly why the

20
00:00:57,000 --> 00:00:59,800
spreadsheet is the wrong tool for this decision. Here is

21
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:02,800
what the spreadsheet leaves out. Paying the mortgage down is

22
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:05,800
a guaranteed return with no sequence risk. Investing is an

23
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:08,800
expected return with a very wide distribution. Those two things

24
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:11,800
are not the same kind of number, and comparing them

25
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:14,800
directly is the mistake almost everybody makes. The second thing

26
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:17,800
it leaves out is liquidity. Money you put into the

27
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:20,800
house is gone until you sell or borrow against it.

28
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:23,800
Money in a brokerage account is there the week your

29
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:26,800
roof fails. And the third thing is the emotional return,

30
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:29,800
which is not a real financial term and is absolutely

31
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:32,800
a real thing. Some people sleep better with no mortgage.

32
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:35,800
That is worth something, and you are allowed to buy

33
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:38,800
it. So here is the order I would actually use.

34
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:41,800
First, get the full employer match. That is not a

35
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:44,800
rate comparison, that is free money, and it wins against

36
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:47,800
everything. Second, build a cash buffer you would not feel

37
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:50,800
embarrassed about. Whatever number makes you willing to leave the

38
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:53,800
extra five hundred dollars invested during a bad year, that

39
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:56,800
is the right number for you. Third, then compare the

40
00:01:57,000 --> 00:01:59,800
rate. If your loan rate is meaningfully above what you

41
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:02,800
expect from the market, the loan is a fine place

42
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:05,800
for the extra five hundred. If it is well below,

43
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:08,800
invest and stop thinking about it. And a practical note.

44
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:11,800
If you do put extra at the mortgage, tell your

45
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:14,800
servicer to apply it to principal. Otherwise a lot of

46
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:17,800
them just park it against next month's payment and you

47
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:20,800
get nothing out of it. Fifteen years of extra payments

48
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:23,800
applied to the wrong bucket is a real thing that

49
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:26,800
happens to real people. What I would not do is

50
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:29,800
agonize. Both of these are good outcomes. You are choosing

51
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:32,800
between guaranteed and probably better, and there is no version

52
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:35,800
of this where you end up worse off than the

53
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:38,800
person who spent the money. Usual disclaimer, I am not

54
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:41,800
a financial advisor and this is not financial advice. If

55
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:44,800
this was useful, let me know which side you landed

56
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:47,800
on. See you next time.

